WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS NOT IN THIS REPO'S OWN SOURCE

Build df8512e0 -- one epoch of off-policy GOLD, granite-4.0-350m from granite-4.1-3b --
completed with every step exiting 0 and produced a broken model: HumanEval 40.9 -> 0.6,
MultiPL-E Java 21.1 -> 0.0, worse on all 30+ benchmarks measured. The cause is the
objective. CustomGOLDTrainer.compute_loss's total loss IS the divergence; there is no term
measuring the student against the corpus tokens, so nothing prices drift away from real
text as long as the student keeps agreeing with the teacher. The student had already been
SFT'd on this corpus family, so it started at JSD 0.0825 against a ln2 = 0.693 ceiling and
banked ~68% of the realisable gain by step 509. For the remaining 7,640 steps the only
descent direction left was to become more certain: entropy fell 42% (0.684 -> 0.396 nats,
branching factor 1.49), reverse KL moved 1.8% while forward KL improved 36%, and the
generations degenerated into repetition loops. The logged loss moved 2.7% across those
steps and showed none of it.

This repo ships no trainer Python. The gold-distill step's run: block execs
$KD_CODE_DIR/gold/gold.py, which constructs CustomGOLDTrainer from the checkout named by
gold_config.kd_code_dir, so no flag, config key or orchestration choice here can add a term
to that loss or log a metric the trainer does not compute. Hence a patch, against a clone
this project controls -- the same treatment and for the same reason as
distill-tokenizer-align/skypilot/patches/retag_student_identity_vocab.diff.

WHAT IT ADDS, ALL DEFAULTED OFF

  ce_coef                       ADDITIVE cross-entropy anchor on the corpus tokens:
                                loss = divergence + ce_coef * CE(student, labels).
  log_student_entropy           mean student entropy and reverse KL per logging_steps.
  entropy_guard_drop_frac       stop when entropy falls this far below its own baseline.
  entropy_guard_baseline_steps  steps averaged before the guard arms.
  entropy_guard_patience        consecutive logs below the floor before tripping.
  entropy_guard_action          "stop" (default) ends the run at the trip step; "warn"
                                reports it, checkpoints it and runs on to max_steps.

A config that sets none of them computes exactly the loss it computed before, which is what
lets the granite4-gold recipes keep their pin untouched.

THREE DECISIONS WORTH THE WORDS

ce_coef is NOT use_ce_loss. That existing flag REPLACES the divergence with cross entropy
(its own help text says "instead of"); this one anchors it. __post_init__ now refuses both
at once, because the two keys read alike and together would apply CE twice while computing
no divergence at all. Additive rather than a convex (1-a)*JSD + a*CE mix because the terms
differ by an order of magnitude on an already-SFT'd pair (JSD ~0.07, CE ~1), so a convex
mix at a=0.5 is ~20x CE-dominated -- SFT with a garnish. Both terms are logged separately,
so the balance is a reading rather than an assumption.

rkld's ORIENTATION follows distill-eval, not convenience:
gb_steps_post_training/distillation/divergence.py computes it as
(exp(log_q) * (log_q - log_p)) with p = TEACHER and q = STUDENT, clamped at zero, on RAW
logits with no temperature scaling. The patch mirrors that exactly, so a per-step rkld is
comparable to the rkld in that step's metrics.json rather than merely similarly named.
Verified equal to that function to within 1e-7 across four chunk sizes, with a
swapped-argument case included to confirm the quantity is not symmetric and the check
therefore proves something.

THE GUARD STOPS, IT DOES NOT RAISE. It is evaluated in log(), on the already all-reduced
mean, so every rank sees the identical number and reaches the same verdict without a second
collective. Raising from compute_loss instead would have to be agreed by every rank inside
the accelerate/DeepSpeed step, and a disagreement there is a hung collective rather than a
stop. It sets should_save -- and, in "stop" mode, should_training_stop -- which Trainer
checks after log() in the same _maybe_log_save_evaluate call, so there is a checkpoint at
the step the guard fired and the process exits 0: the downstream export and eval targets
still run, and the stop reads as a finding rather than a failed build.

STOPPING IS NOT ALWAYS WHAT IS WANTED, WHICH IS WHY entropy_guard_action EXISTS. Build
d1acf1c0 tripped at step 77 of a 2000-step run and stopped there, correctly by its own
rule. But that recipe's purpose is a ladder of exports at 500/1000/1500/2000 measuring how
the collapse develops, so the stop destroyed the data being collected and left all four
export targets naming checkpoints that never existed -- four failed targets, a failed
build, and the run's question unanswered. On a run already bounded by max_steps the horizon
bounds the waste the guard was written to prevent, so there the trip is worth a reading
rather than a stop. "warn" prints the trip step, saves a checkpoint at it, and continues.
Both modes latch after firing once; without the latch "warn" would re-report and re-save on
every subsequent log.

THE CE ANCHOR'S LOG WAS OFF BY gradient_accumulation_steps, fixed here. _ce_anchor_sum
gains one term per microbatch while _ce_anchor_step_eq gains 1/ga, so ce_sum/ce_eq is a
per-optimizer-step SUM while `loss` carries the per-microbatch MEAN. That number exists to
be compared against the divergence in `loss`, so the factor made the one comparison it is
for wrong: on d1acf1c0 (ce_coef 0.05, ga 3) it logged 1.922 where the CE actually added to
the loss was 0.643. The log therefore makes the anchor look THREE TIMES HEAVIER than it
is -- ce_coef * 1.922 = 0.096 against a divergence of 0.0825 reads as an anchor already
outweighing the divergence, when the term truly added was 0.032, well under half of it.
Sizing ce_coef from that reading picks a value three times too small, which is the likely
history of 0.05. Confirmed two ways: the derivation above, and measured against build
bb779f1f, whose ce_coef 0 loss is the pure divergence on the identical first batch.

NOTE THAT THE OBJECTIVE WAS NEVER WRONG. compute_loss adds the correctly normalised
per-microbatch ce_anchor; the accumulators this fixes feed only the log. d1acf1c0 trained
against exactly the anchor its ce_coef asked for, so no run is invalidated by this -- only
the number a human would have read when choosing that ce_coef.

SCOPE: only the same-tokenizer branch of compute_loss. The ULD/cross-tokenizer branch and
the liger fused path are untouched -- neither is used by the recipes this is for, and the
fused path materialises no [B,T,V] logits to run CE on without re-projecting.

WHICH BASE, AND WHY NOT THE SHARED CHECKOUT'S HEAD

/proj/granite-build/g4os/kd-sandbox is another project's tree (origin
github.ibm.com:Takuma-Udagawa/kd-sandbox) carrying 159 uncommitted files, gold/ among them.
Its HEAD reads fc7d66e, which is what df8512e0 recorded as kd_sandbox_commit -- and that
metadata does not describe the code that ran, because the code that ran was working-tree
state. What it does match, byte for byte, is github.ibm.com:karve/kd-sandbox at 20b416c:
every gold/*.py that gold.py loads, plus configs/deepspeed/accelerate_deepspeed_zero3.yaml,
hashes identically there. The single exception is gold/sft.py, which gold.py never imports
(distill-sft-baseline gets its SFT code from the steps collection, not from here). So
20b416c is the nameable commit for what df8512e0 actually executed, and it is the base.

HOW TO APPLY

  git clone git@github.ibm.com:karve/kd-sandbox.git \
            /proj/granite-build/g4os/kd-sandbox-gb
  cd /proj/granite-build/g4os/kd-sandbox-gb
  git checkout -b epic61/ce-anchor-and-entropy-guard 20b416c
  # confirm the base is the tree that ran, before changing anything:
  sha256sum gold/custom_gold_trainer.py gold/custom_gold_config.py gold/gold.py
  #   8aea99d0d32bb1608afd7bb7ba3dd9b2d4c597e96d60723bff9517c30de6bb8d  custom_gold_trainer.py
  #   f297ffe252bb87dab72e0f1cf21dfd2351fd6cb5d18329991cb051b29932579a  custom_gold_config.py
  #   4ffb888d15c4124c403cdcd432d6c61bf4d4191f199d84218917271f5c948268  gold.py
  git apply /path/to/this/file
  git commit -am "gold: anchor the divergence with CE, and log the entropy that collapses"
  #   ...then the second commit, for entropy_guard_action and the ce_anchor_loss scale.

A COMMIT SHA IS NOT REPRODUCIBLE FROM A DIFF. Re-committing this patch produces a different
hash from the `Result` above, because the hash covers author, date and message as well as
the tree. So the two delivery routes are not interchangeable for the pin:

  * bundle (what was actually used) -- `git clone -b epic61/ce-anchor-and-entropy-guard
    kd-sandbox-gb.bundle <dest>` lands commit 09deff2 exactly, and the kd_expect_ref in
    step-template.yaml matches as shipped. BlueVela's login nodes cannot reach
    github.ibm.com over SSH, which is why the bundle exists at all.
  * git apply + commit -- lands the same TREE under a different SHA. Then
    `git rev-parse HEAD` is the value to write into gold_config.kd_expect_ref, and the
    step will refuse the run until it does. Compare trees, not commits, to confirm you
    got the same code:
        git diff --stat 09deff2 HEAD     # empty means the trees agree

Offer this upstream, to gb-steps-distillation now rather than to kd-sandbox: that repo is
what code_config pins. When it lands there and the pin is bumped past it, point the
recipes' CODE_DIR back at "" and delete this file.

